Post-Acute Infection Syndrome
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Persistent profound fatigue >4 weeks post-infection | Post-exertional malaise (PEM) | Complete absence of active/ongoing localized infection

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-015
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of profound systemic fatigue and physical collapse following a minor exertion, requiring urgent exclusion of occult sepsis or acute cardiac failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate severe, sudden-onset exhaustion, palpitations, and near-collapse. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out post-viral myocarditis, pulmonary embolism, or acute adrenal insufficiency, preventing catastrophic physiological collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I walked to the letterbox and my body just completely shut down; my chest is pounding, my brain is in a thick fog, and my joints are screaming.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports persistent, crushing fatigue that dramatically worsens with even trivial physical or mental exertion. There is widespread, migratory musculoskeletal pain and profound brain fog with impaired short-term memory. The patient experiences entirely unrefreshing sleep. Today's acute collapse features orthostatic intolerance, dizziness upon standing, and palpitations without primary cardiac pathology. The patient notes an ongoing post-exertional malaise lasting well over 24 hours from previous mild activities, new-onset headaches, and intermittent low-grade temperature dysregulation.
Functional Status: Completely bedbound during acute post-exertional "crashes."
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe disruption to occupational and domestic life; high spousal caregiver burden.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears profoundly exhausted, speaking in short sentences due to lack of energy, and wearing sunglasses indoors due to photophobia. Medical history notes a recent complex admission characterized by a fever >38.3C for >3 weeks, a lack of localizing signs, and remaining undiagnosed after 1 week of initial investigation before resolving into this chronic post-acute state.
Physical Exam: Cardiopulmonary exam is entirely unremarkable. There are no signs of right heart failure or myocarditis. Musculoskeletal exam reveals diffuse tenderness across multiple muscle groups without true joint effusion or synovitis. Neurological exam reveals delayed verbal processing times but no focal motor or sensory deficits.
Environmental Observations: Curtains drawn, room kept silent and dark.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking numerous over-the-counter supplements.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Acute Infection Syndrome - Status: Severe acute post-exertional exacerbation ("crash").
Differentials:

Acute Myocarditis - Ruled out clinically due to normal SpO2, absence of resting tachycardia while supine, and normal lung auscultation without heart failure signs.

Adrenal Insufficiency (Addisonian Crisis) - Ruled out due to stable normotension, lack of characteristic hyperpigmentation, and absence of acute severe vomiting.

Severe Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out as the primary acute cause due to the distinct physiological markers of post-exertional orthostatic intolerance and specific symptom clustering following a documented infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s severe functional collapse required urgent after-hours assessment to exclude immediate, life-threatening complications following a recent severe systemic illness. Telephone triage identified the critical need to rule out delayed catastrophic events such as post-viral myocarditis or an Addisonian crisis. A rigorous physical evaluation confirmed absolute hemodynamic stability, clear respiratory function, and an absence of focal end-organ failure. This definitive assessment successfully halted an unnecessary and physically destructive emergency department transfer, which would have severely worsened the patient's post-exertional malaise, securing them safely at home under a strict resting protocol.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Implemented strict radical rest protocols (Pacing). The expected investigation results include the absence of acute inflammatory markers with elevated convalescent pathogen-specific IgG titers, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via the regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To avoid triggering severe physiological crashes, implement strict activity pacing by resting completely for ten minutes before and after any minor task; respecting this metabolic envelope will prevent the autonomic nervous system from acutely overloading.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Advised strict use of a shower chair to minimize orthostatic energy expenditure.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for a coordinated multidisciplinary chronic fatigue management plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops unremitting chest pain, sudden severe shortness of breath at absolute rest, or a new focal physical weakness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; post-acute syndromes are complex, exclusion-based diagnoses requiring extended observation.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves severe, unpredictable energy fluctuations; recovery is nonlinear and heavily dependent on aggressive energy conservation rather than graded exercise.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you feel like you are suffocating, or if your heart rate remains violently high and irregular even when you are lying completely flat in a dark room.

PATIENT EDUCATION

